<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/068Z">Warning <span data-tag="068Z">9.3.6.5</span>.</a> In the situation of Theorem <a data-tag="068Y" href="/tag/068Y">9.3.6.4</a>, the assumption $\lambda \trianglelefteq \mu $ cannot be omitted. For example, let $S$ be set of cardinality $\geq \mu $, let $\operatorname{Sub}_{&lt; \kappa }(S)$ denote the nerve of the partially ordered set of all $\kappa $-small subsets of $S$, and define $\operatorname{Sub}_{&lt; \lambda }(S)$ and $\operatorname{Sub}_{&lt; \mu }(S)$ similarly. Assume that $\kappa \trianglelefteq \lambda $ and $\kappa \trianglelefteq \mu $ (this condition is always satisfied, for example, if $\kappa = \aleph _0$: see Example <a data-tag="061Q" href="/tag/061Q">9.1.7.10</a>). Then Example <a data-tag="065B" href="/tag/065B">9.3.2.15</a> supplies equivalences </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }( \operatorname{Sub}_{&lt; \kappa }(S) ) \xrightarrow {\sim } \operatorname{Sub}_{&lt; \lambda }(S) \quad \quad \operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\mu }( \operatorname{Sub}_{&lt; \kappa }(S) ) \xrightarrow {\sim } \operatorname{Sub}_{&lt; \mu }(S). \]
</div>
<p> In this case, the conclusion of Theorem <a data-tag="068Y" href="/tag/068Y">9.3.6.4</a> is satisfied if and only if the comparison map $\operatorname{Ind}_{\lambda }^{\mu }( \operatorname{Sub}_{&lt; \lambda }(S) ) \hookrightarrow \operatorname{Sub}_{&lt; \mu }(S)$ is an equivalence, which is equivalent to the requirement $\lambda \trianglelefteq \mu $ (Example <a data-tag="065B" href="/tag/065B">9.3.2.15</a>). </p>
